A||N||16||CoolBlue87GT||A horror story you've always heard about ....||||4||
Z||000000||CoolBlue87GT||06-21-2001||12:48 AM||||I took the vehicle I use to for towing my Fiero to get it's first "free life time oil changes" today from my Ford dealer.<P>When it was ready, I checked under the hood to make sure they changed the filter. Then I started it up.<P>To my horror, the motor started tapping real loud, and the oil gauge showed zero pressure !! <P>I shut it down quickly, and ran to get the service manager.<P>THEY FORGOT TO PUT IN THE OIL !!!!<P>Holy crap ! They check it, bone dry !<BR>Next they said "lets drive it over to the bay door".<P>I told him to think twice about that.<P>After putting in 5 qts, he restarted it, and the tapping slowly went away. I'm hoping no permanent damage was done.<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000001||fropuff||06-21-2001||12:58 AM||||Pay very close attention to what's going on.  If there is just a slight tick or the oil pressure is a little low, sue the sh*t out of those bastards.  I wish someone would do that to me.  Sounds harsh, but if they know it's their fault, you could get a new engine.  Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pontiaddict||06-21-2001||01:07 AM||||It's a scam!<BR>If they kill the car, they don't have to keep doing free oil changes.<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000003||CoolBlue87GT||06-21-2001||01:11 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fropuff:<BR><B>Pay very close attention to what's going on.... Good luck!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I made darn sure the service manager knew what was going on.  I only bought it last month !!  It a 99 Cherokee Sport, 2 wheel, 4 door.  Very nice shape.<P>I am praying it didn't hurt it.  I only ran it for 5 sec.  The idiot that drove it out of the shop had it running a little longer then that.<P>||||reg||4
Z||000004||CoolBlue87GT||06-21-2001||01:14 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Pontiaddict:<BR><B>It's a scam!<BR>If they kill the car, they don't have to keep doing free oil changes.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I guess life time means 1 oil change !!<P>||||reg||6
Z||000005||cadaver||06-21-2001||02:32 AM||||So you opened the hood to see if they changed the filter but never pulled the stick?||||reg||1
Z||000006||CoolBlue87GT||06-21-2001||07:59 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cadaver:<BR><B>never pulled the stick?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I know !!  I thought about doing it, but thought hey, it's got to be full.<P>Well from now on, you'd better believe it, I'm going to check after every oil change no matter what.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/CoolBlue87GT.gif"> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||StuGood||06-21-2001||08:41 AM||||Those are the kinds of things that make me want to get the technician's name, service manager's name, and write a calm, factual  letter to Ford telling them what occurred... and maybe congratulating them on losing another potential repeat customer.  <P>Actually, it depends a lot on their attitude.  Everyone makes mistakes - God knows I do.  As long as they apologize and <B>try</B> to make things right, you can't really stay mad at them.  <P>But, suggesting they <B>drive</B> the vehicle over to the service bay so they can put the oil in it?!  Hmmm...  let's see, now, they're the trained experts, and you're just a customer...?<P>Oh, well, those are just my off-the-cuff  thoughts on the subject.  I guess you had to be there...||||reg||13
Z||000008||Formula88||06-21-2001||10:09 AM||||While this is still fresh in everyone's mind, I'd get documentation from the Service Manager, the dealership's owner or General Manager, and the Service Tech who did the work that they forgot to put oil in the car, drove the car out of the service bay, and delivered it to you without oil.  Make sure they acknowledge this was their screw up, so if you have any problems down the road, you have proof that it's their fault.  They, of course, won't want to do this, but I'd call Ford and demand a new engine under warranty if they refuse to sign.  Hell, you could drain the oil out and run the engine till it blew, then put oil back in and they couldn't prove it wasn't because of their screw up.  They know this.  That's why they'll try to avoid legally admitting any responsibility.  If you let this go, you have no bargaining power in the event of later problems.||||reg||4
Z||000009||87GTZ34||06-21-2001||10:41 AM||||Sure cadaver hindsight is 20/10... <BR>I agree with formula88 except the fraud part. In addition to getting it properly documented don't admit that it sounds 'normal' (as in the way it sounded when you brought it in).  You always hear the slight tick, right?  This is of course opinion on your part, but it keeps the issue verbally open.  If they don't hear the tick then that is also their opinion. This is important if you have to go to court (or arbitration) because your opinion will be valid since you drive this vehical more. GL||||reg||1
Z||000010||TRiAD||06-21-2001||12:32 PM||||Honestly, as the former Service Advisor at a Ford dealership, I can tell you you have <B>no reason</B> NOT to demand they replace the engine...Immediately. <P>One letter written by an attourney will get you MILES of lee-way with the dealership. <P>I would also threaten to contact ALL the local media if they do not comply. <P>I would NOT wait until the problem is more severe, memories fade, often on purpose. <P>Best of luck. <P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||06-21-2001||12:43 PM||||exactly why i dont do dealerships. Last trip to one cost me over $1,000 in damage. Oh yea, and a statement from the service manager that my car was screwed up when i dropped it off. As far as your oil change, I had a gf take her brand new 1 month old Trans AM for its first change before she went on spring break. 100 miles away the loose oil plug fell out, blew her engine. She had to take them to court, she did get a new motor.||||reg||1
Z||000012||CoolBlue87GT||06-21-2001||10:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B>I can tell you you have [b]no reason</B> NOT to demand they replace the engine...Immediately. <BR>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>When I started it, the noise wasn't there right away. So I think there was enough oil left in the passages. The tech only moved it 20 ft after the "oil drain", so no test drive.<P>After he added the five qts, we started it, and after 3 - 4 seconds it was quite.<P>Since it's not a Ford, I'm sure they would fight against replacing an engine.  I bought it from them with 32K miles.  It's a 99 4dr Cherokee Sport 4.0 L six cyl, two whl drive.<P>We have already documented the event. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||qwikgta||06-21-2001||10:49 PM||||Happened to me too. I went to Jiffy Lube (for my p-up truck) and they did their 29 point check and forgot one....the oil.  The funny thing is that next to the oil was the changer and the checkers initials.  I started it up and drove away before I noticed the oil pressure.  Went back and they made good on the price.  Then I had to go through a battery of tests, they pulled a sample of the oil and had it sent off to see if it contained excess amounts of bering material ect...  In the end there was no prob with the motor....I sold it about 6 months later anyway.<P>ROb<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe, 65K, Header no Cat, 16" GTZ rims, short shifter, swaybar, open element air cleaner, hood vents.<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html<BR>http://vafiero.com||||reg||1
Z||000014||LarryB||06-21-2001||11:07 PM||||Good luck.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <P>I had a dealer (Bonnell Ford in Winchester MA) screwed up replacing the rear main oil seal on the one and only Ford I ever owned; after about 2 miles the engine dumped all the oil. By the time the oil light came on the damage was done, the engine had a severe piston slap.<P>They botched the engine rebuild, and the car was returned a documented 13 additional times over about a 9 month period.  They finally agreed to tear down the rebuilt engine after my lawyer pointed out to them they had an excellent chance of losing the lawsuit being filed (liability for triple damages applied as well) in my behalf; buy that time the engine was burning a quart of oil about every 25 miles.  From memory when they tore the engine down that second time they found that a piston ring had broken.<P>During this whole process Ford would not stand behind their dealers work.  As a result, I'll never buy another Ford. They've lost a potential customer for life.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||CoolBlue87GT||06-21-2001||11:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by qwikgta:<BR><B>Happened to me too. I went to Jiffy Lube ...  In the end there was no prob with the motor<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Wow, you were lucky. That could have been a disaster. The one thing this made me remember, I once had this same engine (258 ci) straight six in a 74 Hornet.<P>It developed a ticking noise, which after replacing the lifters & pushrods, turned out to be from just 1 rocker/pivot assemble.||||reg||1
Z||000016||CoolBlue87GT||06-21-2001||11:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LarryB:<BR><B>Good luck. ...During this whole process Ford would not stand behind their dealers work.  As a result, I'll never buy another Ford. They've lost a potential customer for life.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>As far as I'm concerned, this was a mistake, that should not have happened. The Manager was proactive and satisfied me.  -- Now Back to Fiero world.<BR>||||reg||1
